After refreshing, you see some posts [
    {
        "post_id": 1,
        "user_id": 0,
        "content": "UniversalVault is a macOS menu-bar app that stores structured personal records encrypted on this Mac. Control+Option+V opens a search palette over whatever app you are in; search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field. An item can be marked protected, which seals its fields under a second key and asks for Touch ID before they are read; the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID. Editors autosave and show a Saved badge. An item can be marked for agents, which lets a local command print its non-sensitive fields as JSON. The vault opens at launch without a prompt. Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only; signing out or quitting closes the whole vault. There is no cloud sync.\n\nUse it through a working day: fill a few forms in other apps, add an item, protect one, come back after lunch, and look something up again. Say what happened, what you expected, and what it cost you in time, attention, or trust.\n",
        "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:33:02.948745",
        "score": 2,
        "num_shares": 0,
        "num_reports": 0,
        "comments": [
            {
                "comment_id": 1,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 0,
                "content": "The Control+Option+V palette is exactly what I want. Yesterday I filled four forms by copy-pasting my address from a notes file, and I still managed to paste my phone number into the postcode field once. Search a field, hit Return, done \u2014 that's the entire feature I care about.\n\nWhat I'd actually say after a working day: the plain items flew. Name, address, card, saved plates \u2014 all typed in without me leaving the app. I never opened settings once, which is the point, because I'm not going to.\n\nThe friction was the protected item. I marked my card protected, went to lunch, came back, opened the palette and it just sat there asking for a fingerprint. My assumption with anything that opens at launch is that it's already unlocked for me, so I hit Return a few times before I understood the thing wanted Touch ID. Cost me maybe a minute and a small amount of trust \u2014 \"wait, is this locked or broken?\"\n\nAlso: since I closed the vault window without reading anything, I didn't realise quitting the app closes everything. I quit to free up the menu bar, reopened, and my whole day's sorted items were gone. Autosave with a Saved badge made me confident the second time round, but that first one stung.\n\nHonestly \u2014 give me the shortcut and autosave, and just tell me up front that sleep seals the protected stuff. That's the only setting I need to know about.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:46:30.335325",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 2,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 3,
                "content": "Esto es exactamente lo que llevo buscando para el Mac de casa. Yo uso el m\u00f3vil para todo y el ordenador es el de la familia, as\u00ed que tengo ah\u00ed papeles que no quiero que nadie lea. Me surgen dos dudas: yo cierro la sesi\u00f3n cuando lo dejo, y siempre he dado por hecho que con eso ya queda todo cerrado, \u00bfes as\u00ed? \u00bfla b\u00f3veda entera se cierra al salir? Y si cierro la app porque tengo prisa, \u00bflo que estaba escribiendo se borra de verdad o queda algo por ah\u00ed? Lo de que se abra al arrancar sin pedir nada me da un poco de miedo con un adolescente en casa. Lo probar\u00e9 una ma\u00f1ana y os cuento cu\u00e1nto tiempo me hace perder.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:48:13.550145",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 3,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 1,
                "content": "Threat-model questions before I'd install this, because the copy is doing a lot of reassuring work here.\n\n1. \"The vault opens at launch without a prompt\" \u2014 so before I've done anything, every unprotected record is decrypted into a process I can attach to, readable by anything running as my UID. Is the at-rest file at least sealed with a key held in the Keychain, or is \"encrypted on this Mac\" just a database with its key sitting next to it in Application Support?\n\n2. Protected items: second key plus Touch ID gates reads *in the palette*. Is the item re-sealed on disk when the palette closes, or does the unwrapped key stay in memory for the whole session? \"Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only\" implies the plaintext was sitting there to be sealed \u2014 so what was holding it in the meantime, and for how long?\n\n3. \"Marked for agents\" prints non-sensitive fields as JSON. Who classifies a field as non-sensitive \u2014 me, explicitly, or a default? A field is only non-sensitive until someone redefines the schema or an editor autosaves into it.\n\n4. Autosave and a \"Saved\" badge: is every intermediate state a fresh encrypted revision, or does a temp/plaintext copy ever touch disk or swap? Sleep-then-crash is my usual test and it's the one that finds the leftovers.\n\n5. The palette typing values into the focused field \u2014 does it respect secure input, or does the value travel the general event/pasteboard path where any input-monitoring process can read it?\n\nNone of this is disqualifying; the write-up is unusually honest about launch behaviour, which is a point in its favour. But \"sealed\" is quietly doing the work of \"safe\" here, and they aren't the same claim. I'd want a threat model page, with a stated adversary, not a feature list. Until then the honest headline is: this protects your data from other people's laptops, not from other code on yours.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:53:51.953025",
                "score": 0
            }
        ]
    }
]
